<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Meiji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Meiji Emperor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Meiji was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1867 to 1912. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> and restored imperial rule in his name, and his reign saw rapid modernisation, industrialisation and the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> into a major power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji.jpg" alt="Emperor Meiji" width="281" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uchida Kuichi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meiji_Emperor_(cropped)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Meiji Restoration of January 1868 overthrew the Tokugawa shogunate after some 265 years and restored <b>Emperor Meiji</b> to nominal supreme power, while the Charter Oath of April 1868 committed Japan to seeking knowledge throughout the world and abandoning the customs of the feudal past.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under mounting pressure, the last shogun, Tokugawa Yoshinobu, returned his governing authority to the young <b>Emperor Meiji</b> in November 1867, and the restoration of imperial rule was proclaimed on 3 January 1868.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Meiji</b>, the teenager Mutsuhito, came to the throne when Emperor Kōmei died in February 1867, and the restoration was carried out in his name.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The young <b>Emperor Meiji</b>'s government launched Japan's rapid modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-3">3 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Site of the Nakayama Residence. Died in Meiji Palace. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ye, Our subjects, be filial to your parents, affectionate to your brothers and sisters; as husbands and wives be harmonious, as friends true; bear yourselves in modesty and moderation; extend your benevolence to all…&#8221;</p><footer>Imperial Rescript on Education, 1890 &#183; official English translation, Japanese Ministry of Education, 1907 &#183; issued in his name; drafted by his ministers Inoue Kowashi and Motoda Nagazane &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Imperial_Rescript_on_Education" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Way here set forth is indeed the teaching bequeathed by Our Imperial Ancestors, to be observed alike by Their Descendants and the subjects, infallible for all ages and true in all places.&#8221;</p><footer>Imperial Rescript on Education, 1890 &#183; official English translation, Japanese Ministry of Education, 1907 &#183; issued in his name; drafted by his ministers Inoue Kowashi and Motoda Nagazane &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Imperial_Rescript_on_Education" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
